### Compiler invocation

Use an up-to-date release build of the compiler when one is available. The
`moth` command in PATH is the usual direct form:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    moth check docs --terse
    moth build docs --release
]

If a suitable release build is not available, run the same operations through
Cargo from the repository root:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    cargo run --quiet -- check docs --terse
    cargo run --quiet -- build docs --release
]

### Shared Cargo defaults

`rust-toolchain.toml` selects one exact Rust release with Clippy and rustfmt.
Repository-local Rust commands use that pin regardless of the machine's default
toolchain, and recipes use plain Cargo commands without a separate toolchain or
version check. CI installs the same pin through rustup and never advances it
independently, without a setup action injecting a different
incremental-compilation policy.

Moth tracks current stable Rust closely during alpha development. Advancing the
pin is a deliberate repository change followed by normal validation, and
returning to an earlier release is equally an explicit pin change. Environment
and explicit rustup overrides remain the caller's responsibility. Record
`rustc -vV`, `cargo clippy -V` and `cargo fmt --version` when comparing results.

`Cargo.toml` owns one dev profile, inherited by test: no debug information,
incremental compilation enabled and the normal debug assertions and overflow
checks retained. Release and profiling keep their existing settings. The
workspace warning policy denies warnings for every member in direct Cargo
commands and recipes. It does not change dependency lint policy.

`.cargo/config.toml` defaults Cargo build jobs and Rust test threads to four.
Rayon intentionally keeps its host-selected default unless the caller sets
`RAYON_NUM_THREADS` for a specific command. Environment overrides and explicit
Cargo options remain available. For example, bound all three forms of concurrency
on a smaller host:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=1 RUST_TEST_THREADS=1 RAYON_NUM_THREADS=1 just validate
]

These controls bound concurrency, not aggregate memory. Preserve one effective
configuration during timing comparisons. Changing compiler,
features, profile settings or environment flags can require different artifacts.
Source edits can reuse dependencies and incremental state from earlier builds.

`just clippy` runs `cargo clippy -p moth -p moth-lexical -p moth-mon`.
`just fmt` and `just fmt-check` select the same three packages with `cargo fmt`,
adding `--check` for the latter. Routine recipes keep code moved into the two
standalone libraries covered without enabling extra features or targets.
Broader coverage remains explicit: `just clippy-features` checks the workspace
and all targets under the standard feature set, and `just fmt-workspace-check`
runs `cargo fmt --all --check`. A bare Cargo command uses Cargo's defaults and
does not claim the recipes' explicit package coverage.

### Choose the final gate by scope

Use the smallest checks that cover the change at the current checkpoint.
A commit or slice on a feature branch is not automatically a merge checkpoint.

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Checkpoint**]
        [: **Required validation**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Feature-branch iteration or intermediate commit]
        [: Targeted Cargo checks, filtered tests, selected integration cases or affected feature lanes]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Important feature-branch boundary or broader integration checkpoint]
        [: `just validate`, plus affected checks the routine gate does not cover]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Completed plan or feature in final preparation for merge into main, or release readiness]
        [: `just validate-full` on the final integrated tree]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Every commit landing on main, including documentation-only commits]
        [: Passing `just validate-full` evidence for the resulting tree]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Documentation-only checkpoint on a feature branch]
        [: The documentation gate below, without unrelated compiler suites]
    ]
]

During branch work, use focused checks after each meaningful slice. Use
`just validate` at important boundaries rather than after every small edit.
Reserve `just validate-full` for final plan or feature merge preparation. Even
changes to validation infrastructure, Cargo settings or timer erasure use their
affected commands during intermediate work rather than repeatedly running every
unrelated gate. An explicit user request for a wider run still takes precedence.

Performance-sensitive changes need relevant non-recording evidence when assessing
their effect. Select the affected benchmark or `just validate-perf` when its
coverage is needed, not automatically for every branch commit. Full validation
already includes the standard performance checks.

The main-commit rule applies to the tree actually landing, including merge or
rebase resolutions. After changes invalidate a passing full run, validate the
final integrated tree again before merging. Record the tested revision and build
configuration. The same passing run can serve branch closeout and main entry
when that tree and configuration are unchanged. Intermediate commits kept only
on the feature branch do not each need retrospective full runs.

This cadence governs generic per-slice validation instructions in older plans
and agent workflows. Preserve explicitly required behaviour tests, regression
checks and evidence for the changed owner without expanding each into a full
repository run. Report the selected commands and what they covered. A targeted
or routine pass does not claim that unrun families passed.

The `justfile` owns command contents. `ship`, `release` and release CI use
`validate-full`. This policy changes when to run those commands, not their
coverage or failure contracts.

### Code-bearing final gate

A slice is code-bearing if it changes Rust, compiler or build-system sources,
libraries, tests, benchmarks, manifests, scripts, configuration or any other
non-documentation implementation file.

#### JavaScript runtime prerequisite

Two contracts govern the JavaScript runtime. The language and backend
requirement is Node 24 or newer, or another JavaScript host providing native
`Math.f16round`, because emitted F16 values call that builtin directly. The
repository validation environment is the exact Node release selected by
`.node-version`. GitHub CI installs that release explicitly. For authoritative
local validation, select the repository Node version through a version manager
rather than relying on whichever `node` happens to be installed globally.

The integration lane executes the emitted JavaScript with the `node`
executable found on `PATH`. The integration runner probes the runtime once and
reports the executable and version as a fatal infrastructure failure when it
lacks native `Math.f16round`, instead of letting individual F16 fixtures crash
with engine errors. The probe enforces the capability requirement, not the
exact `.node-version` release.

Run the probe directly to diagnose `PATH` or version-manager mistakes before a
full gate:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    node -p 'process.execPath + " " + process.version + " " + typeof Math.f16round'
]

Use `Fast iteration commands` for small feature-branch slices. At an important
branch boundary, the routine correctness gate is:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just validate
]

Full-plan or feature merge preparation uses `just validate-full` under
`Choose the final gate by scope`. Completing an intermediate slice does not
require that full run.

#### What just validate runs

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Gate**]
        [: **Current command**]
        [: **Purpose**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Formatting check]
        [: `just fmt-check`]
        [: Formatting for `moth`, `moth-lexical` and `moth-mon`, without writing source]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Clippy]
        [: `just clippy`]
        [: Default features and targets for `moth`, `moth-lexical` and `moth-mon`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Feature lane coverage]
        [: `just feature-lane-check`]
        [: Proves every declared Cargo feature has an executing lane, and that no `cfg` names
        a feature that does not exist]
    ]

    [td:
        [: **Rust unit tests**]
        [: `just validate-unit-tests`]
        [: Rust unit and subsystem tests across the complete workspace, using
        the shared Cargo defaults and workspace-unified feature configuration]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Compiler integration tests]
        [: `cargo run --quiet -- tests --terse`]
        [: Canonical suite policy followed by end-to-end compiler cases]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Documentation check]
        [: `cargo run --quiet -- check docs --terse`]
        [: Moth docs-source checking inside the routine gate]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Source audit]
        [: `just source-audit`]
        [: The one broad-source architecture audit: `cfg!` timer checks, direct
        clocks, collector and runtime calls outside the facade, closure
        wrappers, unguarded timer-only fields, obsolete timer macros,
        provisional and hard-coded metric names, and the removed-name
        tripwires. Reports typed findings and writes `source_audit.json`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: First-party dependency audit]
        [: `just first-party-deps`]
        [: Scoped first-party package and runtime roots must contain no package-manager manifests,
        vendored dependency directories or unapproved lexical JavaScript module-loading forms.
        Cargo metadata also enforces the extracted Rust libraries' dependency direction.
        Invalid runtime imports from a registered module are a distinct rule. Host-driven script
        loading is out of scope. The audit writes `first_party_deps.json`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Cache maintenance]
        [: `just cache-maintain`]
        [: After successful checks, prune old timing reports and report a soft
        disk budget while preserving build artifacts and current test reports]
    ]
]

The workspace test command includes the compiler, CLI, both standalone libraries
and `xtask`. It is not the compiler's default configuration.
`xtask` depends on `moth` with
`features = ["timers"]`, and Cargo unifies features across one resolve graph.
The default configuration and each standard feature configuration are covered
by `just test-feature-matrix`, not by `just validate`.

The integration command loads the complete canonical suite and applies the shared hard-policy
evaluator before selection or compilation. This makes missing ownership, duplicate primaries and
other hard suite-policy findings part of the normal validation gate.

#### Performance and full gates

`just validate-perf` runs `bench-ci` and `bench-scaling`, then cache maintenance.
`bench-ci` preflights every standard benchmark case once, then measures the quick
CLI and frontend subsets with three iterations. Scaling fits the declared metric
against input size and fails on a budget violation or unmeasurable series. Both
commands are non-recording. They retain their existing workloads and budgets.

`just validate-full` runs the routine gate, workspace formatting, standard-feature
Clippy, the executing feature matrix, the test honesty audit, performance checks
and the exact no-timer release-artifact check. It finishes with cache maintenance.
Boracle, its campaign, stress and recording benchmarks remain explicit opt-in work.

Standard-feature Clippy checks every workspace target and deliberately excludes
Boracle. Linux, macOS and Windows CI use the same feature selection. The timer
erasure gate builds the no-timer release binary and rejects every timer-only
marker and every name from the schema-owned metric inventory in its bytes.
Routine source audit retains the complementary timer-source rules.

The full gate deliberately covers more than the previous local recipe because
it also executes the feature matrix and honesty audit. Compare identical scopes
when measuring build improvements. Moving release builds out of routine
validation is a scheduling change, not evidence that release compilation got faster.

### Cache maintenance

The `xtask/src/cache.rs` owner queries Cargo metadata for the selected target
directory. It prunes only regular `cargo-timings/cargo-timing-*.html` reports
with recognised timestamp spelling. It retains the newest ten, every report
less than a day old, future-dated reports, the latest-report alias and unknown
files. Symbolic links and Windows reparse points are not followed. A redirected
target or timing-report root is rejected before deletion.

Maintenance reports logical file bytes, with hard links counted per path.
This can differ from allocated disk space reported by `du`. The default soft
budget is 32 GiB, adjustable with positive integer `MOTH_CACHE_BUDGET_GIB`.
Exceeding it prints advice, not automatic Cargo-artifact deletion or a failed
test verdict. Invalid configuration or housekeeping IO errors fail the command.
Maintenance after a failed gate is skipped so failure evidence stays intact.

Run maintenance with other Cargo invocations stopped. Preserve compiler
artifacts, dependencies, incremental state, benchmark workspaces and test reports.
This policy does not impose a hard disk cap or identify every stale Cargo file.
Cargo owns its artifact layout and no per-run timestamp proves an artifact is
unneeded by a later configuration.

For deliberate eviction, stop editor and agent builds sharing this target,
preview the selected files and then apply package-scoped Cargo cleaning:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just cache-evict-preview
    just cache-evict
]

This evicts current dev/test artifacts for `moth`, `moth-lexical`, `moth-mon`
and `xtask`, so the next run pays a rebuild. It preserves unrelated dependencies
and release builds. It is never automatically triggered by the size warning
and may not bring all target usage under budget. Use a one-time clean for a
profile migration, not after each run.

Cargo target overrides apply to maintenance through metadata. Existing benchmark
compiler helpers still assume repository-local `target/release` and
`target/profiling` paths. Use normal per-worktree targets for full validation
until those helpers have a separately verified artifact-path change.

### Test honesty audit

The campaign that hardened this suite is owned by one command:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just test-honesty-audit
]

It writes the canonical inventory to `target/test-reports/test_honesty_inventory.json`, and it is
the single owner of what that file says. The inventory has four parts:

- Hard findings. Shapes with no honest use in this tree: the retired `temp_dir` helper, a `/tmp`
  literal handed to a call that touches the filesystem, a discarded current-directory or
  environment restoration, a discarded thread join, an `ignore` attribute with no reason, and a report
  written straight to its final path. Every hit fails the audit.
- Review findings. Shapes that can hide a weak contract and are also correct in most of their
  uses. The audit counts them, names the files, and records the disposition the campaign decided
  for the category. These never fail the audit. `is_err`, `contains` and `any` are review
  categories on purpose: a gate that failed on each of them would be turned off within a week.
- The declared ledger, read from `docs/roadmap/evidence/honesty_ledger.json`. Every entry must
  carry a severity, a status, a description, a disposition and an owning phase. A duplicate code,
  a missing decision or an open hard finding fails the audit.
- The composed audits. The source audit and the feature-lane check are run, not read, so the
  verdict never depends on when somebody last ran them. The integration suite inventory is read,
  because the compiler binary writes it, and its state is reported as one of absent, unusable or
  present so a missing report can never be read as a clean one.

The source scan walks `src`, `crates/moth-lexical/src`, `crates/moth-mon/src`,
`crates/moth-mon/tests` and `xtask/src`. Nested subsystem test directories are
included recursively. Missing configured roots fail the audit. The console and
inventory name the roots scanned by the run.

The tracked durable copy under `docs/roadmap/evidence/` is refreshed by the same owner, never by
hand:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just test-honesty-evidence
]

### What a report guarantees

Every machine-readable report is written to a sibling temporary file and renamed into place, so a
reader never observes a partial file and a failed write leaves the previous report intact.

Every report also carries the identity of the run that produced it: an id unique within the
process without depending on the wall clock, the command, the OS and architecture, the features
the linked compiler was built with, a thread count where the command owns one, and whether the run
finished.

Completion state is what separates the reports, and the distinction is real:

- `test_honesty_inventory.json`, `source_audit.json`, `first_party_deps.json` and
  `integration_suite_inventory.json` are replaced by a `completed: false` report before their owned
  work starts. A run interrupted partway leaves a report that says so rather than a previous
  successful one that looks current.
- `feature_matrix_results.json` starts with every standard lane `pending` and is rewritten as each
  standard lane resolves, so an interrupted matrix reports the lanes it measured and the lanes it
  never reached. Opt-in lanes are owned by their named commands and do not appear as unmeasured
  matrix outcomes.
- `feature_lane_coverage.json` is a coverage map, not an outcome table. It answers which lane
  covers which declared feature, which `just feature-lane-check` establishes without running a
  single lane, so it is complete the moment it is written and never states a lane result. Lane
  outcomes live in `feature_matrix_results.json`.

### Source audit

An architecture ban whose scope is "no file anywhere may contain this" is not a behaviour claim,
and a unit test that reads one file's text and asserts on it is not behaviour evidence. Those bans
have one owner:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just source-audit
]

It walks `src`, `crates/moth-lexical/src`, `crates/moth-mon/src`,
`crates/moth-mon/tests` and `xtask/src` once, including nested subsystem tests,
and reports typed findings with the file each was found in. The console and
JSON report list those roots. Missing configured roots fail the audit. Only the
audit's own implementation files are exempt, because they necessarily contain
the fragments the rules search for. Their rules are proved against fixture text
instead. A source file that cannot be read is a finding, not a file the audit skips.

Add a broad-source ban here rather than as a test. A ban that lives in a test reads one file, calls
that evidence, and stops applying the moment the code moves.

### First-party dependency audit

The first-party package promise has one focused owner:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just first-party-deps
]

It walks only these first-party implementation roots: `packages/`,
`src/builder_surface/core_packages/`, `src/backends/js/package_bindings/` and
`src/projects/html_project/binding_packages/`. It also inspects the compiler-owned JavaScript
inventory from `moth::first_party_js`: Core helper bodies, `@core/collections` runtime helpers,
inline lowering templates and every module registered in `RuntimeModuleRegistry`.
JavaScript module-loading classification is owned by the HTML JS parser scanner. This audit maps
those diagnostics onto two rules and does not keep a second lexer. Classification is lexical and
covers the forms the scanner parses: static `import` declarations including a clause-less
side-effect import, dynamic `import()`, CommonJS `require()` and re-export `from` clauses.

The only allowed JavaScript module specifier is an exact compiler-owned runtime module registered
by `RuntimeModuleRegistry`. Dynamic `import()`, `require()`, relative, absolute, URL and data
imports are rejected. Exact package-manager manifest and lockfile basenames, and `node_modules`,
`vendor` and `third_party` directory names, are rejected under the scoped roots.
Invalid named imports or unsupported import forms from a registered runtime module are reported
as a distinct runtime-import rule, not as an unapproved third-party module specifier.

Host-driven script loading is outside this audit: classic-worker `importScripts`, `new Worker(url)`,
an injected `script` element and specifiers reaching `eval`, `new Function` or a `fetch` response.
Those are arbitrary runtime behaviour rather than a declared dependency, so no lexical scan can
bound them, and teaching the scanner call-expression semantics would buy a false-positive surface
without closing the hole. The promise holds there because first-party JavaScript is compiler-owned
either as a physical asset under the audited roots or as an inventoried fragment in compiler
sources, generated `runtime_glue` is emitted from those owned sources, and both routes reach the
repository through review.

This source walk does not include generated `runtime_glue`, user-owned or future dependency packages,
documentation, test fixtures, benchmarks or repository-root manifests. Missing roots,
unrepresentable paths, non-file/non-directory entries and unreadable JavaScript sources fail
closed rather than being treated as clean.

The same command reads `cargo metadata --no-deps --format-version 1` for the
Rust workspace. Across normal, development and build dependencies,
`moth-lexical` may depend on no first-party workspace package and `moth-mon`
may depend only on `moth-lexical`. Neither may depend on `moth` or `xtask`, even
when that package is outside workspace membership. Renaming a
dependency does not change its package identity or exempt its edge. Missing
configured packages and failed metadata queries fail closed. The console and
report list the inspected Rust manifests alongside the JavaScript roots.

### Feature lanes

`just validate` runs one standard feature configuration. The standard lanes run all standard
configurations:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    just test-feature-matrix
]

Ten package-scoped standard lanes cover the compiler's default configuration,
`timers`, `detailed_timers`, `benchmark_counters`, `timers` with
`benchmark_counters` (including the retained-layout memory probe), the scoped-block
feature lane, every `show_*` feature, `xtask` and feature-free runs of
`moth-lexical` and `moth-mon`.
`testing.mtf > Feature and platform lanes` owns the lane table and each lane's coverage.

The `boracle` feature is an opt-in developer lane. `just boracle` owns its formatting check,
feature-enabled Clippy and smoke test. The `boracle-campaign` lane, using `boracle_campaign`, is a
separate opt-in developer lane owned by `just boracle-campaign`. It was measured at roughly 61
seconds against roughly 8 seconds for the default lane, so it cannot belong in the default lane.
Both lanes are intentionally excluded from `just validate`, `just validate-full`,
`just test-feature-matrix` and ordinary CI gates.

Lanes use `cargo test -p <package>` rather than `--workspace` because Cargo unifies features across
one resolve graph. `xtask` requires `moth` with `timers`, so no workspace-wide command can run the
default configuration.

`just feature-lane-check` proves the mapping without running a lane, and is part of `just validate`.
The report distinguishes standard and opt-in lane owners. Adding a Cargo feature means adding it
to a standard or opt-in lane in the same change. The check fails otherwise.
Run affected executing lanes for feature-gated work even when the routine gate passes.
Use the relevant package, feature set and test filter during a branch slice.
Run the complete standard matrix at full-validation checkpoints rather than
for every intermediate feature-branch commit.

### CI gates

`just validate` is the routine local gate and is deliberately fail-fast: the first failure stops
the run. `just validate-full` provides all standard families as one fail-fast command.
The per-main-commit requirement is independent of how frequently CI is triggered.
A push containing several main commits does not establish full evidence for each
one merely because CI tested its final revision.

CI reports each validation family as its own job, so one failure never stops
another from reporting:

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Gate**]
        [: **Command**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `format`]
        [: `just ci-gate-format`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `clippy`]
        [: `just ci-gate-clippy`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `unit-tests`]
        [: `just ci-gate-unit-tests`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `feature-matrix`]
        [: `just ci-gate-feature-matrix`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `integration`]
        [: `just ci-gate-integration`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `docs`]
        [: `just ci-gate-docs`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `benchmarks`]
        [: `just ci-gate-benchmarks`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `scaling`]
        [: `just ci-gate-scaling`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `timers-erasure`]
        [: `just ci-gate-timers-erasure`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `source-audit`]
        [: `just ci-gate-source-audit`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `first-party-deps`]
        [: `just ci-gate-first-party-deps`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: `honesty-audit`]
        [: `just ci-gate-honesty-audit`]
    ]
]

Each gate runs on Linux, macOS and Windows. Every gate consumes the Rust release pinned by
`rust-toolchain.toml`. The `unit-tests`, `feature-matrix` and `integration` gates execute rendered
JavaScript and also install the Node release pinned by `.node-version`. The workflow result is still
failed when any blocking gate fails. The `integration`, `feature-matrix`, `first-party-deps` and
`honesty-audit` gates
upload `target/test-reports/` whether they passed or failed, because a failed run's report is the
evidence a reviewer needs most.

`unit-tests` and `feature-matrix` overlap on purpose. `unit-tests` runs the workspace-unified
command a developer runs locally. `feature-matrix` runs the configurations that command cannot
reach.

The release workflow uses `just validate-full`. The split gates report on pushes
to main and manual workflow dispatch. Creating a PR does not itself trigger this
workflow, and no CI result may be claimed without an actual completed run.

### Platform policy

Linux and macOS gates are blocking. Windows gates are non-blocking: a Windows failure is reported
but does not fail the workflow or hold back deployment.

Windows becomes blocking when both hold:

- every gate has passed on `windows-latest` in one run of the default branch
- no Windows-only failure is open in the exposed-failure ledger

The change is then removing `continue-on-error` from the gate job. Until that happens, a Windows
result is evidence to act on, not a gate that can be ignored: a Windows-only failure belongs in the
ledger like any other.

A platform-specific test may be absent only when the underlying API does not exist on that
platform. An equivalent portable policy test should still run everywhere. `testing.mtf > Feature
and platform lanes` owns which gate owns which platform behaviour.

### Documentation-only release-build gate

A slice is documentation-only only when every modified file is documentation
source or generated documentation.

Allowed examples include:

- files under `docs/**`
- an explicitly requested root documentation file such as `README.md`,
  `CONTRIBUTING.md` or `AGENTS.md`
- generated files under `docs/release/**`

A documentation-only slice must not change Rust, tests, libraries, build
configuration, manifests, scripts, benchmark code or fixtures, compiler
configuration or other executable project sources.

During documentation-only branch iteration, inspect the changed source, links
and examples, using `moth check docs --terse` when checking Moth docs sources.
At a documentation completion checkpoint on that branch, run one documentation
release build using a current compiler.

The direct release-binary form is:

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    moth build docs --release
]

If no suitable release build is available, use
`cargo run --quiet -- build docs --release` instead.

Then:

- inspect every changed route
- inspect the generated diff
- verify links, tables and code examples
- confirm generated output came from source changes
- confirm the changed-file list contains documentation only.

This is sufficient for a documentation-only feature-branch checkpoint. Leave
unrelated Clippy, unit, integration and benchmark suites for the appropriate
wider checkpoint. A Cargo invocation of the compiler is an alternative way to
run the same documentation gate, not an extra gate.

Final merge preparation and every commit landing on main still require
`just validate-full`, including documentation-only changes. The branch-only
exception does not waive that requirement. If any non-documentation file
changed, select code-bearing checks under `Choose the final gate by scope`.

### Fast iteration commands

Prefer the narrowest useful command during feature-branch work. A compile check
is useful early feedback but does not replace behaviour tests when behaviour
changed. Package and test filters avoid rebuilding or running unrelated targets.

`testing.mtf > MON and shared lexical coverage` owns standalone codec test
locations and the real-source parity harness. Its `gaps.rs` keeps intentional
data/source differences separate from accepted but undelivered source `{=}`,
Unicode escapes and contextual `::Variant`. A filtered parity run must execute
the intended cases. A pass with zero selected tests proves nothing.

[table:
    [th:
        [: **Task**]
        [: **Command**]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Compile-check one package]
        [: `cargo check -p <package>`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Focused Rust test iteration]
        [: `cargo test -p <package> <filter>`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Shared identifier and numeric-text rules]
        [: `cargo test -p moth-lexical`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Standalone MON codec and public API]
        [: `cargo test -p moth-mon`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: MON crate documentation examples]
        [: `cargo test -p moth-mon --doc`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Shared lexical crate documentation examples]
        [: `cargo test -p moth-lexical --doc`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Real-source Moth/MON syntax parity]
        [: `cargo test -p moth --lib mon_syntax_parity`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Focused feature-gated tests]
        [: `cargo test -p moth --features <features> <filter>`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Complete workspace tests when needed]
        [: `just validate-unit-tests`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: One integration case]
        [: `cargo run --quiet -- tests --case <id> --terse`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: An affected integration family]
        [: `cargo run --quiet -- tests --tag <tag> --terse`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Complete integration suite when needed]
        [: `moth tests`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Clippy iteration]
        [: `cargo clippy` or `just clippy`]
    ]

